    "narrative_title": "Oil Surge, Persistent Inflation, and a Fed-Driven Rate Shock",
    "narrative_summary": "A converging shock of higher oil prices and stickier-than-expected inflation is driving central banks—especially the Fed—toward renewed and repeated rate hikes, which in turn is re-pricing bond yields, borrowing costs and risk assets. Treated as an open system, the situation has multiple inflows (oil supply disruptions, CPI prints, fiscal deficits), internal processes (monetary policy decisions, bond market repricing, corporate financing adjustments) and outflows (higher mortgage and sovereign borrowing costs, equity volatility, consumer spending squeeze) with feedbacks that can amplify or dampen the cycle. Key dynamic linkages include oil → inflation → policy → yields → real economy, plus fiscal and geopolitical shocks that modify supply and market expectations.",
    "scope": "U.S. and major global financial markets (equities, bonds, FX), central bank policy decisions (Fed focus), inflation and CPI dynamics, oil and energy supply shocks, mortgage and consumer borrowing costs, sovereign and corporate debt markets",
